// Interactive logins with no session recording behind them.
//
// This is the query that matters most in this directory. Every other one reads
// the recordings; this one looks for the sessions that should have produced a
// recording and did not, which is the only way a silent failure in the shell
// substitution becomes visible.
//
// Filters, in order:
//   1. Logins: sshd session-open records from Syslog over the window. The
//      username is pulled out of the message with a regular expression, because
//      sshd writes it into the text and not into a field.
//   2. Service accounts and the accounts that are deliberately not recorded are
//      excluded here, from one list, so the exclusion is visible in the output of
//      the query rather than buried in a join.
//   3. Recordings: the tlog session fragments over the same window, reduced to
//      one row per user and host with the times it was recording.
//   4. A left outer join on host and user, then a time tolerance: a login is
//      recorded if a tlog session on the same host for the same user was running
//      at the time, allowing toleranceMinutes of slack for the shell substitution
//      to start writing. A login with no such session is a finding. The tolerance
//      is why this is not a plain left anti join on host and user: that form
//      suppresses every login on a host for anyone recorded there even once,
//      which hides exactly the failure this query exists to find.
//
// Known false positives and gaps when this was written:
//   - A non-interactive "ssh host command" opens and closes a PAM session without
//      a login shell, so it never produces a recording. It is a true absence and a
//      false finding, and the only reliable way to separate it from a real gap is
//      whether a shell was allocated, which the sshd message does not say.
//      Expect these, and expect them to be the bulk of the output on a host that
//      anything automated logs into.
//   - scp and sftp behave the same way, for the same reason.
//   - A login by a user in a group outside tlog_record_groups is not a failure. It
//      is the policy working, and it is why unrecordedAccounts exists.
//   - A recording that started and was then dropped by journald rate limiting
//      produces fragments, so it passes this query while being incomplete. That
//      failure is invisible from here; it is why the rate limits are configured
//      off in the tlog role rather than monitored.
//
// Tune toleranceMinutes first if this is noisy. Raising the window does not help.
let window = 7d;
let toleranceMinutes = 5m;
let unrecordedAccounts = dynamic([
    "example-svc-backup",
    "example-svc-monitoring",
    "root"
]);
let logins =
    Syslog
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(window)
    | where ProcessName == "sshd"
    | where SyslogMessage startswith "Accepted" or SyslogMessage has "session opened for user"
    | extend LoginUser = extract(@"(?:for user |for )([A-Za-z0-9._@-]+)", 1, SyslogMessage)
    | where isnotempty(LoginUser)
    | where LoginUser !in (unrecordedAccounts)
    | project LoginTime = TimeGenerated, Computer, LoginUser
    // One id per login, so the join below can fan out over every recording for
    // that user and host and then be collapsed back to one row per login.
    | serialize LoginId = row_number();
let recordings =
    Syslog
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(window)
    | where ProcessName == "tlog-rec-session"
    | extend rec = parse_json(SyslogMessage)
    | extend RecordedUser = tostring(rec.user)
    | where isnotempty(RecordedUser)
    | summarize RecordingStart = min(TimeGenerated), RecordingEnd = max(TimeGenerated)
        by Computer, RecordedUser, SessionId = tostring(rec.session);
logins
| join kind=leftouter (
    recordings
    | project Computer, LoginUser = RecordedUser, RecordingStart, RecordingEnd
  ) on Computer, LoginUser
| extend CoveredByThisRecording = iff(
    isnotnull(RecordingStart)
      and LoginTime between ((RecordingStart - toleranceMinutes) .. RecordingEnd),
    1,
    0
  )
| summarize Covered = max(CoveredByThisRecording) by LoginId, LoginTime, Computer, LoginUser
| where Covered == 0
| summarize UnrecordedLogins = count(), FirstSeen = min(LoginTime), LastSeen = max(LoginTime)
    by Computer, LoginUser
| extend Note = "Login with no tlog session covering it on this host. Check whether a login shell was allocated before treating it as a recording failure."
| order by UnrecordedLogins desc
